  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Sunday)\nIssued at 1147 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nThe pattern will remain stagnant over the next week with a sprawling \nand strong upper level high either overhead or nearby. Temperatures \nwill remain consistent, with highs in the upper 90s to low 100s each \nday and lows mostly in the 70s. Dew points will remain on the lower \nside through Tuesday (though perhaps slowly rising with time), so\neven though temperatures will be hot, heat indices will only run \nslightly higher than the temperatures. This may keep heat products\nconfined to the typically hotter locations. This is already the \ncase for Monday, with a pair of Heat Advisories for portions of \nNortheast OK and west-central AR. \n\nBy mid week the low level flow will become more S-SSE instead of S-\nSSW which will help low level moisture creep upwards. The increase \nin dew points may expand the footprint of heat products at that \ntime. The chance for rain was already pathetic, and it looks even \nlower now for the mid week, with only a 5-10 percent chance of a few \nhigher terrain diurnal showers. Models increase the chance of \nsimilar showers or storms to 10-20% over the higher terrain of \nnorthwest AR next weekend with the upper level high sitting just \nwest of the area. With continued hot, dry, and breezy weather, \nsome fire weather potential will exist each afternoon throughout \nthe region.",
